00:01:01.575 --> 00:01:03.015
That's really all that matters.

00:01:03.015 --> 00:01:03.545
Which may or...

00:01:03.545 --> 00:01:07.875
which may be true in fact is true,
but most people are very concerned

00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:09.465
about their own pronunciation.

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:13.905
Can you give us some background on
your own journey in so far as your

00:01:13.905 --> 00:01:19.065
own very North American pronunciation
and the kind of advice and help

00:01:19.095 --> 00:01:22.205
you, you give people around this
whole subject of pronunciation?

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:23.820
Of course.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.880
So I was born and raised in Israel.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.910
I was never exposed to English growing up.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:36.540
And when I was 21, I wanted, I decided
to move to New York to become an actress.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.550
And uh, I showed up with
my intermediate English.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:47.604
I, you know, I definitely had a very
noticeable Israeli accent where, you know,

00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:49.345
people would ask me, where are you from?

00:01:49.615 --> 00:01:53.005
You know, Hadar, such an exotic
name, and like, there was always a

00:01:53.005 --> 00:01:58.965
conversation about me and my accent
and, um, which was okay, like I,

00:01:58.995 --> 00:02:01.005
I, you know, it is what it is.

00:02:01.425 --> 00:02:03.345
And then I studied acting.

00:02:03.735 --> 00:02:07.785
I started an acting school and there
we learned speech and pronunciation.

00:02:07.785 --> 00:02:12.645
And I fell in love with this idea
of modifying my sounds just by

00:02:12.645 --> 00:02:14.115
learning what to do with my mouth.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.204
And everyone learned speech and
pronunciation, native speakers

00:02:17.204 --> 00:02:20.325
and non-native speakers, 'cause
we learned classical American.

00:02:20.745 --> 00:02:22.065
Uh, that was the first year.

00:02:22.065 --> 00:02:28.065
The second year we studied different
dialects and what it did is it totally

00:02:28.065 --> 00:02:35.984
opened up my mind in terms of not only
my, uh, how I sounded, but also it

00:02:35.984 --> 00:02:38.115
changed my confidence, how I showed up.

00:02:38.115 --> 00:02:40.785
I understood so much more how
to use the language better.

00:02:41.055 --> 00:02:42.734
I felt more effortless.

00:02:43.215 --> 00:02:46.875
Um, I had more confidence because
every time, you know, before I

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:54.010
would second guess myself or avoid
saying certain words that had th

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.710
or r and that stopped happening.

00:02:57.859 --> 00:03:02.329
When I made the new sounds my
new habit, my new speaking habit.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:04.000
Um...

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.140
A couple of questions.

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:06.620
Sorry, go ahead.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:07.550
No, I'll let you finish.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:08.450
No, no, go ahead.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.019
All the things you say sort of, uh,
makes me think of questions, but I

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:13.700
can save my questions so you go ahead.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:14.630
But that's it.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.870
But then, After the two years of acting
school, I already had a pretty solid

00:03:19.929 --> 00:03:23.590
American accent and I was able to shift
between accents and I learned it, and

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.489
I knew how to, you know, I, I learned
the different sounds and gradually

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:36.285
moving back to Israel, I realized that
no one here talked about pronunciation

00:03:36.285 --> 00:03:40.155
and I realized how important it is
for building confidence and fluency.

00:03:40.335 --> 00:03:41.505
Not just to sound an Amer...

00:03:41.595 --> 00:03:42.585
like an American, right?

00:03:42.585 --> 00:03:46.305
But mostly to build that confidence
speaking a second language.

00:03:46.305 --> 00:03:47.565
So I started teaching it.

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:54.255
Okay, so the first question that comes
to mind is, an actor by definition

00:03:54.255 --> 00:03:56.984
is someone who is not inhibited.

00:03:56.984 --> 00:04:00.375
You know, you, you're acting, you're
pretending to be someone else,

00:04:01.065 --> 00:04:02.954
uh, which is a particular mindset.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.720
Uh, and so you're used to the idea
that you're gonna imitate this

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.420
person, you're gonna imitate their
role in the story, you're gonna

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.460
imitate the language they use.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:17.340
So I think an actor has a big advantage,
uh, in learning languages and also has,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.500
um, you know, they're more confident.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.000
So to what extent can you take that
experience of an actor, you, the

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.570
boudding actor, actress, whatever
the word is today, and that

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.550
confidence that you obviously had.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:33.930
And how do you then transport
that over to your average person?

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.830
Ah, this is such a good question
'cause I talk about it a lot.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:43.800
Like me learning, acting, and making
someone else's words my own is a big

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.420
part of the experience of a language
learner because all of a sudden you take

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.530
someone else's words or someone else's
language and you wanna make it your own.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:59.910
So you want to let go of what you hold so
closely, like your own pronunciation and

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:04.260
how you perceive the language and your
own rhythm, and you need to surrender to

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.870
the new sounds and the new intonation.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:12.330
So there is this aspect of letting go
that is oftentimes so hard for people.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:17.670
So I think that the mindset work
around what it means to speak a second

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:24.375
language, letting go, trying out
different things, exploring your voice.

00:05:24.375 --> 00:05:29.715
When I teach, I use a lot of, um,
techniques from my acting studies, you

00:05:29.715 --> 00:05:34.095
know, using voice, understanding how
to, how to play with your voice, and

00:05:34.095 --> 00:05:39.599
also letting go of the, of the, of the
tension that you have when speaking.

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:42.299
So I absolutely think it's the same.

00:05:42.299 --> 00:05:46.409
I think that even as an actress,
I had a lot of challenges with

00:05:46.414 --> 00:05:49.919
confidence being one of the only
non-native speakers in class.

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:55.200
So I always had issues with,
with my pronunciation and things

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.369
that would be so easy for others,
I had to work harder for them.

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:04.169
But I think that has taught me all
the things that I know now and you

00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:05.570
know, got me to where I am today.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:08.805
You know, it's very interesting.

00:06:09.104 --> 00:06:14.775
Uh, an actor imitates, uh, uh, you know,
projects into the role of the person.

00:06:15.255 --> 00:06:20.474
Uh, when we learn languages, we are
imitating an aspect of another culture.

00:06:20.955 --> 00:06:23.895
We're actually trying to
project into that culture.

00:06:24.284 --> 00:06:29.835
Um, Uh, the best language learners, they
see themselves as members of that group.

00:06:29.865 --> 00:06:31.245
I learn French.

00:06:31.245 --> 00:06:31.965
I want to be French.

00:06:31.965 --> 00:06:32.625
I learn Chinese.

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.555
I want to be Chinese.

00:06:34.155 --> 00:06:36.435
Uh, I don't hold back in my own culture.

00:06:36.705 --> 00:06:40.665
And I've often said that to learn
a language you need to experience

00:06:40.665 --> 00:06:43.160
a certain cultural weightlessness.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.970
In other words, you're not an
Israeli person speaking English,

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.160
you're an American, I'm a Chinese.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:51.620
Uh, easier said than done.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:58.100
How are you able to get people
to let go of their identity uh,

00:06:58.105 --> 00:07:02.210
and, and just without fear, just
kind of throw themselves in?

00:07:02.215 --> 00:07:03.170
Because it's the unknown.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:07.530
You're throwing yourself into something
that you're, it's not familiar to you.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.980
And for any number of reasons
people, oh, as they get older, they

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.219
don't like to change, they don't
like to abandon their identity.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.740
The, you know, actors do
that, that's their profession.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.690
But how do you get
ordinary people to do that?

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:24.460
I think it's all about accepting the
fact that it's going to be different.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:28.120
They're not gonna feel completely
like themselves at the beginning.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:32.980
And the ideal is to bring it to a place
where you feel authentic and you feel

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:38.680
like yourself, but the path, the road
there will require you to discover new

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:43.120
things about yourself, to discover, uh,
you know, you, you want to say something,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.780
you have all these thoughts running
around your head in your native language.

00:07:47.789 --> 00:07:52.650
And what you're able to portray is a
fraction of it and you need to be okay

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.520
with it, and to find a way to express
yourself and to connect and to communicate

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:02.940
with others with whatever tools you have
and I think that if you understand that

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.850
and you're willing to, to be okay with the
experience that it's not gonna be the same

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:12.390
and you can't, you cannot compare yourself
to your native language all the time.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:13.870
Not in how you sound.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:15.150
Not in your pronunciation.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.990
Not in, not in how you are able to express
yourself, 'cause it's just not fair.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.890
The circumstances are different.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.720
And people constantly do that and then
they're like, I'm not good enough.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:26.310
I feel like a fraud.

00:08:27.065 --> 00:08:32.075
And having those thoughts is definitely
limiting them and preventing them

00:08:32.075 --> 00:08:35.585
from taking action, speaking more,
'cause they're constantly focused

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:38.920
on what other people are gonna say,
but if you just operate from...

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:39.700
yeah.

00:08:41.455 --> 00:08:44.645
I was just gonna say, what you are saying
now is that it's not just pronunciation,

00:08:45.125 --> 00:08:48.845
it's, it's the whole range of, of, you
know, operating in another language

00:08:48.905 --> 00:08:51.425
and, and that obviously as we progress.

00:08:52.185 --> 00:08:54.465
We're never perfect, but we're
getting better all the time.

00:08:54.465 --> 00:08:57.375
So at an earlier stage in our
journey, we're not very good at

00:08:57.375 --> 00:08:59.265
all, and yet we have to keep going.

00:08:59.835 --> 00:09:02.205
Uh, but so it's just, it's
not just pronunciation, it's,

00:09:02.205 --> 00:09:04.885
it's the whole experience of
learning in another language.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.325
We have to accept what we are at
each stage and try to enjoy it.

00:09:08.444 --> 00:09:12.405
Yeah, it's never just pronunciation
or just grammar or just vocabulary.

00:09:12.405 --> 00:09:14.535
It's everything together
and the experience together.

00:09:14.535 --> 00:09:19.035
And you also need to balance between those
things as you're learning and as you're

00:09:19.064 --> 00:09:25.080
focusing, you know, there are stages
in your language learning experience,

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.560
where the focus is, is going to be more
on pronunciation because you've already

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.050
done a lot of work around comprehension.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:37.000
Usually you start learning by
perceiving the language by consuming

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:42.775
the language so that happens naturally
as you develop, as you learn, but

00:09:42.775 --> 00:09:48.395
there is a point where you are ready
to do more work around pronunciation.

00:09:48.395 --> 00:09:51.865
Maybe not very early on, but maybe
there is a stage where you're

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:55.495
ready for it and then that should
be your focus and that is okay.

00:09:55.495 --> 00:09:57.985
And then you can't focus on too
many things at the same time.

00:09:58.345 --> 00:10:06.805
But I agree, it, it's also, um,
it's also complex yet, yet clear.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:10.380
Uh, if you know what it is that
you need at a certain stage.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:18.870
So do you have a range of techniques that
you teach, uh, your subscribers, learners?

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:23.310
In other words, enabling them to
get to a level of better language

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:24.630
usage, better pronunciation.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:28.260
Are there techniques there or is
it all the sort of the attitude?

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.560
So when it comes to teaching
pronunciation, there's

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.610
absolutely a technique.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.110
The first one is perception.

00:10:34.199 --> 00:10:36.420
To be able to make a sound,
you have to hear a sound.

00:10:36.719 --> 00:10:40.295
If you can't hear the difference
between sheep and ship, you're not

00:10:40.295 --> 00:10:42.335
gonna be able to make those sounds.

00:10:42.335 --> 00:10:43.825
So perception is first.

00:10:44.865 --> 00:10:48.815
Then you need to learn
how to pronounce it.

00:10:49.115 --> 00:10:52.305
'Cause sometimes you might hear that
there is a difference between "ee"

00:10:52.325 --> 00:10:56.765
and "i", but every time you try to
say it, it's gonna sound the same.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:00.485
And that's where the technical
aspect comes into play, where you

00:11:00.485 --> 00:11:04.735
learn the difference between what
your intuitive pronunciation is.

00:11:04.835 --> 00:11:08.975
For example, as an, as a Hebrew speaker,
I would just want to say "ee" in both

00:11:08.975 --> 00:11:11.105
cases instead of "ee" versus "i".

00:11:12.425 --> 00:11:21.005
So I need to understand my, what
my, um, default sound is and how to

00:11:21.005 --> 00:11:28.020
shift to be able to, to pronounce the
sounds that I'm aiming to pronounce.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:31.050
And then it's all about
repetition, building habits.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:32.510
Right?

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.520
So, so, so you build the muscle
memory so you don't always have to

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.840
think about it when you're speaking.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.920
And then there is a stage that I
believe is also very important,

00:11:40.925 --> 00:11:42.660
which is intentional speaking.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:46.740
And then you use the specific sounds while
speaking, because usually what happens,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:50.695
even when you repeat it, it's a strategy.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:51.855
That's what I have done.

00:11:51.855 --> 00:11:55.965
I just drilled sounds to be
able to build those habits.

00:11:56.775 --> 00:12:04.305
But then if I want it to be faster than
what I need to do is to also focus on

00:12:04.305 --> 00:12:06.495
speaking or repetition or shadowing.

00:12:06.825 --> 00:12:08.085
Thinking about those sounds.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:12.630
And when I do or I'm more focused
and I'm directing my tongue and

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.680
mouth and lips to go into the
direction that I want it to go.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:20.910
And then there is an overall
strategy that I think is important

00:12:20.915 --> 00:12:25.470
is to understand what sounds are
important for the specific speaker.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.570
'Cause not all sounds
are equally important.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:34.170
For example, the flap T may not be
as critical as someone who is re

00:12:34.469 --> 00:12:36.449
replacing the R and the L, right?

00:12:36.449 --> 00:12:41.820
So if you pronounce the L instead of an
R, that is more critical than the flap T.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.339
Let's say if you pronounce
it like a true T, like a "Tu"

00:12:44.344 --> 00:12:46.050
sound, Betty instead of Betty.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:51.689
So it's not equally important because one
will affect your clarity and the other is

00:12:51.689 --> 00:12:54.660
just, you know, you sound more American.

00:12:55.065 --> 00:12:56.955
So that is also important to understand.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.965
And, and yeah but there are
English speakers who say, Betty.

00:12:59.445 --> 00:12:59.955
So I mean it's not...

00:12:59.955 --> 00:13:03.615
and exactly, it's an allophone,
so that's not even critical.

00:13:03.645 --> 00:13:05.035
But let's say, let's take the R.

00:13:05.355 --> 00:13:09.405
Let's say I say "right"
instead of "right"or "right".

00:13:09.405 --> 00:13:13.575
I use the ... then people
at some point would get it.

00:13:13.665 --> 00:13:15.015
Of course, it affects clarity.

00:13:15.015 --> 00:13:18.435
It's important to work on it,
but people will understand it.

00:13:18.435 --> 00:13:23.235
But if you say "light" instead of "right",
then it's gonna be critical and it will

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.490
for sure affect your, uh, intelligibility.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:33.810
So intelligibility of course is
sort of the, the minimal condition

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:38.490
and then the ultimate is to
be almost taken for a native.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:40.325
How close can people get?

00:13:41.955 --> 00:13:43.410
And does it depend on the individual?

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:48.530
I think it depends on the individual,
the time invested, their willingness

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:52.949
to, to get into that character.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:53.550
Right?

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.449
Because a lot of times
there is resistance.

00:13:55.469 --> 00:14:01.620
I just had a conversation with students
of mine that said when they use the

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:06.520
English sounds instead of their native
sounds, they have this voice that says,

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.430
oh, why are you trying to be so fake?

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:09.150
It sounds fake.

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:10.110
It feels fake.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:15.570
So also being able to let go of that
voice and understand that while it

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:19.380
might feel fake at the beginning,
you can make it your own with, you

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:24.241
know, just like repetition and using
it enough times until you own it.

00:14:25.665 --> 00:14:25.785
Hmm.

00:14:26.355 --> 00:14:28.815
Uh, you know, that's a very good point.

00:14:28.965 --> 00:14:34.275
Um, I'm, I'm reminded of my father who
was born in Czechoslovakia, well became

00:14:34.275 --> 00:14:35.955
Czechoslovakia Austro-Hungarian Empire.

00:14:36.555 --> 00:14:39.375
And in Canada there's a
province called Nova Scotia.

00:14:39.689 --> 00:14:43.560
There's even a Bank of Nova Scotia, he
would always say Bank of Nova Scotia.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.880
Uh, he can say the "shu" sound.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:51.449
He had a wonderful command of
English, you know, vast vocabulary.

00:14:51.599 --> 00:14:53.219
But he would always say Nova Scotia.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.459
At some, at some level, he
didn't want to say Nova Scotia.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:00.120
You know, it's like in Czech,
this would be Nova Scotia,

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:01.680
therefore it should be Nova Scotia.

00:15:01.949 --> 00:15:04.979
There is a level at which
people simply don't want to.

00:15:05.749 --> 00:15:10.140
So I, I I think that, you know,
or they don't enjoy, you know,

00:15:10.569 --> 00:15:15.569
basically becoming more native-like,
so it, it's also a choice.

00:15:15.575 --> 00:15:16.830
I think to some extent.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:21.780
It's absolutely a choice, and I think
it really is all about the experience

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:25.490
of the learner with the language, with
the speaker, the native speakers of

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:30.450
that language, and, um, And I think
it's, it's also having the right

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:34.710
strategy and technique 'cause it does
require commitment and repetition.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:41.460
It's not enough to listen to pronunciation
lesson and accept or, and not expose

00:15:41.465 --> 00:15:43.740
yourself to the sounds of English.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:48.230
And expect yourself to just use
it naturally and spontaneously

00:15:48.235 --> 00:15:49.140
without thinking about it.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:50.400
It just doesn't work that way.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:57.420
You know, uh, I will leave a link to your
website, to your, uh, YouTube channel.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:01.170
And, uh, I agree with what you're
saying, and it begins with perception.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:05.040
But then we need, if we have a specific
goal, then we need to work towards it.

00:16:05.045 --> 00:16:08.310
And you obviously have some excellent
techniques based on your acting,

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:11.819
uh, experience and, uh, your
experience with developing your

00:16:11.819 --> 00:16:13.170
wonderful North American accent.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:16.050
So, uh, you know, uh, I
don't want to cut it off.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:19.860
Sometimes it seems like I cut off these
discussions that we could go on for

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:22.470
another, you know, half hour or so.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:26.040
But maybe we could leave it there because
I think we've hit, you have hit on

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:29.700
some very important points and I would
encourage, uh, my listeners to go to

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:37.515
your YouTube channel and, uh, Hadar,
uh,  Accent's Way if they are interested

00:16:37.515 --> 00:16:40.845
in improving their pronunciation in
English or really the, I'm sure the same

00:16:40.845 --> 00:16:42.735
principles apply to other languages.

00:16:42.765 --> 00:16:43.065
Right.

00:16:43.515 --> 00:16:44.085
And we're gonna

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:48.585
also have an interview with you on
my channel where you shared a lot

00:16:48.585 --> 00:16:53.635
of interesting, um, facts and more
information about language learning so

00:16:53.755 --> 00:16:55.555
we can invite them to watch it there.

00:16:56.965 --> 00:17:00.895
And, and one final point that you
made in our discussion that is there

00:17:00.895 --> 00:17:04.345
may be areas where people who have
different opinions, even people

00:17:04.345 --> 00:17:06.115
who are interested in languages.

00:17:06.204 --> 00:17:08.785
And I think that's the wonderful
thing about language learning.

00:17:08.785 --> 00:17:10.075
It's an individual journey.

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:12.090
People do what they wanna do.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:15.030
They can listen to a variety
of opinions, some of which may

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:16.710
agree, some of which may disagree.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:19.440
Although I found in our
discussion that mostly we agreed.

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:20.340
I think so too.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:21.180
Absolutely.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.840
Okay, Hadar, thank you very much.

00:17:24.845 --> 00:17:25.660
Thank you, Steve.

